  "title": "Add hmac-md5 to packet-isis-clv algorithm_vals",
  "description": "RFC5310 says it \"can be used to specify any authentication algorithm for authenticating and verifying IS-IS PDUs.\"\n\n\"Any\" includes the original HMAC-MD5 algorithm also supported by RFC 5304 although it is not one of the examples listed in [RFC 5310 Section 3.4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5310.html#section-3.4)). \n\nWhen using RFC 5310, different authentication algorithms are identified by their length and in Wireshark the list is encoded in `algorithm_vals` in `packet-isis-clv.c`\n\nHMAC-MD5 ISIS authentication using RFC 5310 (rather than RFC 5304) is used by default with, for example, Nokia SR OS routers and on Juniper routers if configured with the non-default option `isis-enhanced`\n\nWhen looking at a packet generated by one of the above routers with tcpdump, the length 16 HMAC-MD5 digest  is displayed correctly:\n\n```plaintext\n            Authentication TLV #10, length: 19\n              Generic Crypto key-id: 10, password: 6e9e07a0459f5ed0f11aa101c41b6ca8\n              0x0000:  0300 0a6e 9e07 a045 9f5e d0f1 1aa1 01c4\n              0x0010:  1b6c a8\n```\n\nHowever, Wireshark reports `illegal message digest format`\n\n```plaintext\n    Authentication (t=10, l=19)\n        Type: 10\n        Length: 19\n        Key ID: 10\n        CRYPTO_AUTH (3) illegal message digest format\n```\n\nIf the commit in this request is merged, Wireshark no longer reports that the message format is \"illegal\" and the message digest matches the tcpdump output.\n\n```plaintext\n    Authentication (t=10, l=19)\n        Type: 10\n        Length: 19\n        Key ID: 10\n        CRYPTO_AUTH hmac-md5 (3), message digest (length 16) = 6e9e07a0459f5ed0f11aa101c41b6ca8\n```",
  "state": "merged",
